e, coverage, or fixture\\nowner. Route any D98 defect back to N22; do not write terminal code here. Never\\nwiden repair or weaken a denominator.\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPROVED IMMUTABLE PATCH OVERLAY ---\\nPatch ID: P-N31-001\\nReason: N90's audit (finding F1) found D16-D23 fully implemented but never wired into the one production compiled graph, because N31's own module docstring deferred registration to N32 while N32's prompt deferred it right back. graph.py is now in N31's write set to close this ownership gap directly\\nExpected effect: D16-D23 become real, wired members of the compiled production graph; the six N31-owned DEFERRED_EDGES rows resolve; a real compiled fake-transport run reaches UNIT_ACCEPTED; binding_inventory()'s return value stays unchanged so N30's frozen tests keep passing\\nInstructions:\\nOwnership correction from N90's independent audit (finding F1). N90 found that\\nD16-D23 (the unit repair/acceptance cycle you implement) are fully implemented\\nand function-level tested, but never registered or wired into the one\\nproduction compiled graph (`build_curriculum_factory_graph()` in\\n`runtime/langgraph_factory/graph.py`). A real run can never reach\\n`D22_ACCEPT_UNIT`/`UNIT_ACCEPTED` for any unit \\u2014 only the isolated function\\ncalls your own tests exercise prove anything.\\n\\nRoot cause: your own `repair.py` module docstring says \\\"that registration is\\nN32's write\\\" \\u2014 deferring wiring to N32, who deferred it right back (N32's own\\nprompt says the workbook engine is \\\"not called by `build_curriculum_factory_\\ngraph`... N32 owns wiring this function into whatever the production compile\\npoint becomes once the workbook branch is in scope\\\"). Nobody wired D16-D23.\\n`graph.py` is now in your write set specifically to close this. Wire it here,\\nas the node that owns the repair/acceptance node bodies and is best placed to\\nprove them reachable.\\n\\n## What \\\"wired\\\" means and how the existing machinery already does