{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdyVE8N2X1hzPb1z4LiC9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is a genuinely hard architectural tension: wiring D16-D23 for real necessarily changes what the compiled production graph's edges are, but one frozen N30 test (`test_the_compiled_graph_registers_every_declared_unit_branch`) computes its expectations from the narrow `binding_inventory()` while comparing against the *same* real compiled graph — a structural conflict, not a bug. Given the stakes (previous attempt burned $10+ and got interrupted without resolving this), let me get a second opinion from Codex before committing to an approach."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2706,"cache_read_input_tokens":112363,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2706},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b21865dd-1a72-43f4-8cf1-c4fe980203a9","uuid":"089d78ff-0201-426a-bc92-be952c73ca93","timestamp":"2026-08-12T20:30:41.028Z","request_id":"req_011CdyVE7oo5Ne9WP9sQD7xQ"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdyVE8N2X1hzPb1z4LiC9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TwHrwQYq3auaXEFa6ZtUaw","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Second opinion on N30/N31 graph wiring conflict","subagent_type":"codex:codex-rescue","prompt":"Repo path for all investigation: /Users/filipepinto/Projects/curriculum_builder/.plan26-run/plan26/attempts/N31_REPAIR_ACCEPTANCE/attempt-20260812T202440.205845Z-fce9d2b124/repo\n\nContext: this is a LangGraph-based curriculum factory (plan 26). Node N30 built `runtime/langgraph_factory/unit_graph.py` (frozen, not editable) and its test `tests/runtime/test_plan26_unit_graph.py` (also frozen, not editable — it's an independent verification file for the current node, N31). Node N31 is implementing `runtime/langgraph_factory/repair.py` and `runtime/langgraph_factory/acceptance.py` (D16-D23, the unit repair/acceptance cycle) plus wiring them into `runtime/langgraph_factory/graph.py`'s one production compile point `build_curriculum_factory_graph()`. This wiring work is authorized by an approved patch overlay (P-N31-001) whose full text you should read at the top of `runtime/langgraph_factory/graph.py` history isn't needed — I'll summarize the key constraint below.\n\nThe patch's \"Expected effect\" line states: \"D16-D23 become real, wired members of the compiled production graph; the six N31-owned DEFERRED_EDGES rows resolve; a real compiled fake-transport run reaches UNIT_ACCEPTED; binding_inventory()'s return value stays unchanged so N30's frozen tests keep passing.\"\n\nThe current WIP (left by a prior interrupted attempt at this same node) implements this by:\n- `runtime/langgraph_factory/graph.py`: added `unit_repair_binding_inventory()` = `binding_inventory()` (unchanged, narrow) + `acceptance.UNIT_REPAIR_NODE_BODIES` (D16-D23). `build_curriculum_factory_graph()` now calls `register_skeleton(builder, unit_repair_binding_inventory())` instead of the narrow `binding_inventory()`, then calls `acceptance.register_unit_repair_path(builder, sorted(bindings))` afterward to wire D16-D23's own internal sub-cycle edges (D17->D18->D19->...->D16, etc.).\n- Because `register_skeleton` internally calls `unit_graph.register_unit_path(builder, sorted(bindings))` (frozen N30 code, do not touch) with the now-WIDE bindings, the 6 previously-deferred edges (D08_VALIDATE_DOMAIN -> D17, D09_VALIDATE_CONTENT -> D17, D12_VISUAL_BARRIER_AND_JOIN -> D17, D14_INVENTORY_AND_INSPECT_UNIT_PAGES -> D17, D91_CLASSIFY_MODEL_FAILURE -> D17, M05_REVIEW_ACTUAL_UNIT -> D16) become real conditional-edge destinations automatically (this is exactly how `unit_graph.register_unit_path`'s `branch_destinations()` is documented to work: it intersects each source's declared destinations against whatever `available` sequence is passed in).\n\nNow here's the problem I found empirically. `tests/runtime/test_plan26_unit_graph.py` has two module-scoped fixtures:\n```python\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\")\ndef compiled() -> Any:\n    output_root = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=\"plan26-n30-\"))\n    return G.build_curriculum_factory_graph(engine_root=REPO_ROOT, output_root=output_root)\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\")\ndef topology(compiled: Any) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    return G.compiled_topology(compiled)\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\")\ndef available() -> tuple[str, ...]:\n    return tuple(sorted(G.binding_inventory()))\n```\nAnd the test:\n```python\ndef test_the_compiled_graph_registers_every_declared_unit_branch(topology, available) -> None:\n    registered: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n    for source, target, conditional in topology[\"edges\"]:\n        if conditional:\n            registered.setdefault(source, set()).add(target)\n    for source, _guard in U.UNIT_BRANCHES:\n        expected = set(U.branch_destinations(source, available))\n        assert expected, f\"{source} has no registerable destination\"\n        assert registered.get(source) == expected, source\n```\n`compiled` is built via the REAL, actual production entrypoint `G.build_curriculum_factory_graph(...)` — i.e. it reflects whatever bindings that function actually passes to `register_skeleton`/`unit_graph.register_unit_path` internally. `available` is independently recomputed from the narrow `G.binding_inventory()` (which the patch says must stay unchanged, and it does — I verified its return value is untouched).\n\nThe test asserts that for EVERY source in `unit_graph.UNIT_BRANCHES` (which includes D08_VALIDATE_DOMAIN, D09_VALIDATE_CONTENT, D12_VISUAL_BARRIER_AND_JOIN, D14_INVENTORY_AND_INSPECT_UNIT_PAGES, D91_CLASSIFY_MODEL_FAILURE, M05_REVIEW_ACTUAL_UNIT — exactly the 6 rows N31 needs to resolve), the REAL compiled graph's registered destinations for that source equal `branch_destinations(source, available)` computed from the OLD NARROW available set (i.e. WITHOUT D16/D17 as valid destinations, since D16-D23 aren't in `binding_inventory()`).\n\nI ran this test against the current WIP and it fails exactly as I'd predict: `D08_VALIDATE_DOMAIN` now has `D17_CLASSIFY_UNIT_FINDINGS` as a real registered destination in `compiled` (correct, required by the patch), but `expected` (computed from the narrow `available`) does NOT include D17, so `registered.get(source) == expected` fails.\n\nI confirmed via `inspect.getsource(StateGraph.add_conditional_edges)` that LangGraph's `StateGraph.branches[source]` is keyed by the path function's `__name__`, and calling `add_conditional_edges` twice for the same source with the SAME path function name raises `ValueError: Branch with name ... already exists`. So `unit_graph.register_unit_path` (frozen, single source of truth for these edges, called exactly once inside `register_skeleton`) can only ever be called once per build, with one fixed `available` set — there's no way to \"widen after the fact\" for a specific source without either (a) passing the wide set into that single call (which is what causes this exact test failure), or (b) somehow adding a second conditional-edge branch under a different path-function name for the same source (untested whether LangGraph would even execute two branches per source sanely, and this smells like it would violate \"do not touch unit_graph.py\" in spirit since it'd duplicate/fight its single-branch-per-source design).\n\nI confirmed by checking out the ORIGINAL committed version of this repo (before this patch's wiring work, commit 691e176 in the main curriculum_builder repo) that with the narrow (unwired) `build_curriculum_factory_graph()`, all 93 tests in `test_plan26_unit_graph.py` pass. So this is a genuine, clean regression caused specifically by wiring D16-D23 into the one production compile point — not a pre-existing failure, and not (as far as I can tell) fixable by a smarter binding-composition trick, because the conflict is structural: the same `compiled` object is asserted against two different definitions of \"available\" (one wide, for real wiring; one narrow, frozen in the test) that are now provably different for these 6 sources specifically.\n\nI found 7 currently failing tests total when running `pytest tests/runtime/test_plan26_repair_acceptance.py tests/runtime/test_plan26_unit_graph.py`. 6 of them are in the frozen `test_plan26_unit_graph.py` and all stem from this same root cause (either directly, like the one above, or indirectly — other tests use `_build_episode_fixture`/`_run_episode` with a single-unit fixture whose M05 review scripted response has no blocking findings, so now that D16 actually executes for real, the denominator legitimately passes on the first try and the run proceeds all the way through D22_ACCEPT_UNIT/D23_CHECKPOINT_ACCEPTED_UNIT and loops back to a second D05_SELECT_NEXT_UNIT call, which finds the manifest exhausted and routes to the still-deferred D24 — whereas before D16-D23 were wired, the run used to stop dead at the M05->D16 deferred edge, which several of these frozen tests hard-coded assumptions around, e.g. asserting `state[\"selected_unit_id\"] == \"U001\"` post-run, or `deferred_frontier == \"D16_REDUCE_UNIT_EVIDENCE\"`). The 7th failure is in `tests/runtime/test_plan26_repair_acceptance.py` (which IS in N31's write set) — a test the prior interrupted attempt apparently hadn't finished getting to pass yet; I haven't dug into that one's root cause yet.\n\nMy question: do you agree that the 6 `test_plan26_unit_graph.py` failures are a genuine, unavoidable structural consequence of correctly wiring D16-D23 into the real production compile point (i.e., there is no clever binding/registration trick that makes both \"D08's real compiled edges include D17\" and \"this frozen test's hardcoded narrow-`available`-based expectation for D08 still equals the real compiled edges\" simultaneously true) — meaning the right move is to proceed with the wiring, let these 6 tests regress, and document this explicitly and prominently in the node's result report as an intentional, necessary consequence of closing finding F1 (rather than trying to contort the implementation to avoid it, or conclude the whole wiring approach is wrong)? Or do you see an approach I'm missing — e.g. a different LangGraph mechanism, a different binding composition, or a reason my read of `add_conditional_edges`'s single-branch-per-source-name behavior is incomplete — that could let a real compiled `build_curriculum_factory_graph()` genuinely route D08->D17 etc. while this exact frozen test still passes unmodified? I am NOT asking you to write code — just give me your independent analysis and a recommendation, in under 400 words. If you want to look at any file directly (`runtime/langgraph_factory/graph.py`, `runtime/langgraph_factory/unit_graph.py`, `tests/runtime/test_plan26_unit_graph.py`) to verify my claims, feel free — but don't modify anything."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2706,"cache_read_input_tokens":112363,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2706},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b21865dd-1a72-43f4-8cf1-c4fe980203a9","uuid":"22b0425e-4934-44a8-9d15-6d48c3530779","timestamp":"2026-08-12T20:31:17.670Z","request_id":"req_011CdyVE7oo5Ne9WP9sQD7xQ"}
