er_skeleton internally calls unit_graph.register_unit_path(builder, sorted(bindings)) (frozen N30 code, do not touch) with the now-WIDE bindings, six previously-deferred edges (D08_VALIDATE_DOMAIN -> D17, D09_VALIDATE_CONTENT -> D17, D12_VISUAL_BARRIER_AND_JOIN -> D17, D14_INVENTORY_AND_INSPECT_UNIT_PAGES -> D17, D91_CLASSIFY_MODEL_FAILURE -> D17, M05_REVIEW_ACTUAL_UNIT -> D16) become real conditional-edge destinations automatically, per unit_graph.register_unit_path's documented branch_destinations() intersection behavior.\n\nEmpirically confirmed problem: tests/runtime/test_plan26_unit_graph.py has module-scoped fixtures 'compiled' (built via the REAL production entrypoint G.build_curriculum_factory_graph(...)) and 'available' (independently recomputed from the narrow G.binding_inventory()). The test test_the_compiled_graph_registers_every_declared_unit_branch asserts, for EVERY source in unit_graph.UNIT_BRANCHES (which includes exactly the 6 sources above), that the REAL compiled graph's registered destinations for that source equal branch_destinations(source, available) computed from the OLD NARROW available set (i.e. WITHOUT D16/D17 as valid destinations). Running this test against the current WIP fails exactly as predicted: D08_VALIDATE_DOMAIN now has D17_CLASSIFY_UNIT_FINDINGS as a real registered destination in compiled (correct, required by the patch), but expected (computed from the narrow available) does NOT include D17, so registered.get(source) == expected fails.\n\nAlso confirmed via inspect.getsource(StateGraph.add_conditional_edges) that LangGraph's StateGraph.branches[source] is keyed by the path function's __name__, and calling add_conditional_edges twice for the same source with the SAME path function name raises ValueError: Branch with name ... already exists. So unit_graph.register_unit_path (frozen, single source of truth for these edges, called exactly once inside register_skeleton) can only ever be called once per build, with one fixed available set.\n\nConfirmed by checking out the ORIGINAL committed version of this repo (before this patch's wiring work, commit 691e176 in the main curriculum_builder repo) that with the narrow (unwired) build_curriculum_factory_graph(), all 93 tests in test_plan26_unit_graph.py pass. So this is a genuine, clean regression caused specifically by wiring D16-D23 into the one production compile point.\n\n7 currently failing tests total when running pytest tests/runtime/test_plan26_repair_acceptance.py tests/runtime/test_plan26_unit_graph.py. 6 of them are in the frozen test_plan26_unit_graph.py and all stem from this same root cause (directly, or indirectly via _build_episode_fixture/_run_episode tests whose single-unit fixture now legitimately runs all the way through D22_ACCEPT_UNIT/D23_CHECKPOINT_ACCEPTED_UNIT instead of stopping dead at the previously-deferred M05->D16 edge, breaking hard-coded post-run assertions like state['selected_unit_id'] == 'U001' or deferred_frontier == 'D16_REDUCE_UNIT_EVIDENCE'). The 7th failure is in tests/runtime/test_plan26_repair_acceptance.py (in N31's write set) — not yet root-caused by me.\n\nQuestion: is it true that there is no clever binding/registration trick that makes both '(a) the real compiled build_curriculum_factory_graph() routes D08->D17 etc. for real' and '(b) tests/runtime/test_plan26_unit_graph.py's frozen test_the_compiled_graph_registers_every_declared_unit_branch, unmodified, still passes' simultaneously true — i.e. is this a genuine structural conflict given LangGraph's single-branch-per-source-name behavior in add_conditional_edges, unit_graph.register_unit_path being frozen and called exactly once inside register_skeleton, and the frozen test independently recomputing 'available' from the narrow binding_inventory()? Or is there a real mechanism you can point to (different LangGraph API, different binding composition strategy, a way to make unit_graph.register_unit_path itself narrow while a SEPARATE compiled StateGraph or a wrapper node achieves the wide D08->D17 routing without ever calling add_conditional_edges twice for the same source name, etc.) that would let both hold at once? Also separately: root-cause the 7th failing test in tests/runtime/test_plan26_repair_acceptance.py.\n</task>\n\n<structured_output_contract>\nRespond in under 400 words total (verification of my empirical claims can be brief, most of the budget goes to the analysis and recommendation). Structure as:\n1. Verification: confirm or correct my empirical claims by reading runtime/langgraph_factory/graph.py, runtime/langgraph_factory/unit_graph.py, runtime/langgraph_factory/acceptance.py, and tests/runtime/test_plan26_unit_graph.py directly. Flag anything I got wrong.\n2. Structural verdict: yes/no — is this a genuine unavoidable conflict, or is there a real mechanism that reconciles both constraints? If a mechanism exists, name it concretely (API/pattern) — do not hand-wave.\n3. Recommendation: what should the N31 implementer do next (e.g. proceed with wiring and document the 6 regressions as an intentional, necessary consequence of closing finding F1 in the node's result report; or a specific alternative approach).\n4. Separately: root cause of the 7th failing test in tests/runtime/test_plan26_repair_acceptance.py, with a one-line fix recommendation.\n</structured_output_contract>\n\n<default_follow_through_policy>\nThis is a read-only analysis and research task. Do not modify any files. Do not attempt to fix the failing tests. Only investigate, verify, and answer the question above.\n</default_follow_through_policy>\n\n<grounding_rules>\nGround every claim in code you actually read from the repo paths above. If you assert something about LangGraph's internal behavior (e.g. add_conditional_edges branch-name keying), cite the specific source location/behavior you observed. Do not speculate about LangGraph internals without checking the installed package source if needed. If you are not fully certain about a claim, say so explicitly rather than stating it as fact.\n</grounding_rules>\" --wait","description":"Forward read-only analysis task to Codex","timeout":600000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13472,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13472,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TwHrwQYq3auaXEFa6ZtUaw","session_id":"b21865dd-1a72-43f4-8cf1-c4fe980203a9","uuid":"ca7e3072-b1f9-485f-9d8c-dc8ca052c1c8","timestamp":"2026-08-12T20:31:48.466Z","request_id":"req_011CdyVLCaT5xpfbZkYDGJQT","subagent_type":"codex:codex-rescue","task_description":"Second opinion on N30/N31 graph wiring conflict"}
